-- nothing in the bath is redundant, and now there are three independent ways it decides rather than two.
-- Drop the temperature and cyclohexane's counts collapse into one. Drop the handedness and glycerol's do,
-- where the two baths hold the same substance in the same amount and differ only in whether it is one
-- enantiomer or both. Drop the amounts and glucose's do, where the two baths hold the same substances and
-- differ only in how much. The three compounds are a solvent, a solvent and a sugar, and the three
-- quantities are a temperature, an enantiomeric excess and a concentration -- none of them a property of
-- any molecule in the bath.
bath-is-irredundant : (a : count-bath m-cyclohexane (bath-room 2 sol-water) ≡ count-bath m-cyclohexane (bath-cold 2 sol-water) → Empty) × ((b : count-bath m-glycerol (bath-room 0 sol-pirkle) ≡ count-bath m-glycerol (bath-room 0 sol-racemic) → Empty) × (count-bath m-glucose (bath-room 1 sol-trace-base) ≡ count-bath m-glucose (bath-room 1 sol-base) → Empty))
bath-is-irredundant = (m-temperature-decides , (m-handedness-decides , m-concentration-decides))
